TI  - Bioactivity of Beauveria bassiana (Hypocreales: Cordycipitaceae) and Metarhizium anisopliae (Hypocreales: Clavicipitaceae) on Diatraea saccharalis (Fabricius, 1794) (Lepidoptera: Crambidae) eggs.
PG  - 108685
LID - S0022-2011(26)00161-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jip.2026.108685 [doi]
AB  - The use of synthetic insecticides for pest control in agriculture has raised concerns about their negative impacts on ecosystems. In this context, Integrated Pest Management (IPM) has incorporated entomopathogenic fungi as a sustainable alternative to synthetic insecticide application. Beauveria bassiana and Metarhizium anisopliae are widely used in IPM programs to control the larval stages of insects such as Diatraea saccharalis, a lepidopteran that affects sugarcane production in various regions worldwide. However, few studies have addressed the control of this pest during its embryonic development, which may represent a critical stage for population management. This study aimed to analyze the bioactivity of the entomopathogenic fungi B. bassiana isolate IBCB 66 and M. anisopliae isolate IBCB 425 on D. saccharalis eggs through observational, histochemical, and ultrastructural analyses. For this, five concentrations ranging from 5.36 10(5)-5.36 10(7) conidia/mL for B. bassiana and 8 10(6)-8 10(8) conidia/mL for M. anisopliae were used. Both fungal isolates interfered with embryonic development, with B. bassiana and M. anisopliae reducing egg viability by up to 48.25% and 78.5%, respectively. At 144-h post-application, fungal growth was observed on the egg surface, along with melanization and vesiculation at the chorionic periphery, absence of embryonic development, and the presence of amorphous granular structures. The results demonstrate that B. bassiana and M. ani
