TI  - Regulatory roles of three Zn(II)(2)Cys(6) cluster-coding genes in insect pathogenicity and environmental fitness of Beauveria bassiana.
PG  - 106856
LID - S0048-3575(25)00569-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.pestbp.2025.106856 [doi]
AB  - Numerous transcription factors (TFs) with Zn(II)(2)Cys(6) binuclear cluster DNA-binding (C6 or GAL4) domain are generally underexplored in filamentous fungi. Here, 202 C6 TFs with GAL4 domain are verified in Beauveria bassiana and sorted into three subclasses with distinctive domain architectures. Three representative C6 TFs (138, 359, and 1117 aa) with an N-terminal GAL4 domain alone were proven to be regulators of insect pathogenicity, named Rip1-3. Single-gene deletions caused severe attenuation of virulence against Galleria mellonella via normal cuticle infection. The normal infection was blocked since cuticle-degrading enzymes accumulated heavily in hyphae but were hardly secreted out for cuticular penetration, indicating an essentiality of Rip1-3 for hyphal invasion into the host. All deletion mutants were compromised in conidial viability and tolerances to high osmolarity, oxidation, cell wall perturbation and UVB damage, and altered in carbohydrate epitopes on conidial surfaces, unveiling an importance of Rip1-3 for the fungal adaptation to host environment. Despite insignificant changes in aerial conidiation, all the mutants also exhibited sharp increases in blastospore yield, indicating negative roles for Rip1-3 in mediating submerged blastospore production. There were 1523-2678 genes dysregulated in three deletion mutants, including 469-1248 co-dysregulated in paired mutants and 452 co-dysregulated in the three mutants. Notably, numerous co-dysregulated genes were
