TI  - Structure and expression of -1,3-glucan recognition proteins from the ghost moth, Thitarodes pui (Hepialidae), and their response to Beauveria bassiana infection.
PG  - 1660-9
LID - 10.1016/j.jinsphys.2011.08.019 [doi]
AB  - Beta-1,3-glucan recognition proteins (GRPs), as a class of pattern recognition receptors, are involved in activation of the immune response in invertebrates. We cloned two cDNAs encoding putative GRPs from larvae of Thitarodes pui, a host species of Ophiocordyceps sinensis with great economic importance in the Tibetan Plateau. The two putative GRPs were phylogenetically classified into a novel clade 4, and designated TpGRP-4a and TpGRP-4b, respectively, with calculated molecular masses of 53,265 and 43,991 Da. Both TpGRPs contained a C-terminal domain with sequence similarity to -1,3-glucanases but without the glucanase active site. TpGRP-4b markedly differed from other family members including TpGRP-4a in the N-terminal region by a large deletion of 80 amino acid residues. Homology modelings revealed an eight-stranded -sandwich fold (1-8) and two -strands (only 1 and 2), respectively, in the N-terminal domains of TpGRP-4a and -4b. TpGRPs showed similar developmental expression patterns in fat body. TpGRP-4a and -4b transcripts were induced highest 313- and 16-fold, respectively, in resistant 8th instar larvae challenged with conidia of entomopathogenic fungus Beauveria bassiana. By contrast, significant reductions in TpGRPs expression were observed in conidia-injected susceptible 6th instar larvae (compared with saline-injected controls), accompanied by production of hyphal bodies in hemolymph. These results suggest that TpGRPs might contribute to host
